  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a novel hybrid optimization strategy combining frequency-domain analysis with sparse representation in the spatial domain. The core idea is to represent h(x) as a weighted combination of sinusoidal functions, which provides global structure, and then optimize its discrete representation. This approach leverages the Fourier basis to control the function's global properties and then refines a sparse grid representation to satisfy local constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h(x) using complex Fourier coefficients:\n   h(x) = \u2211_{k=-N}^{N} a_k e^{2\u03c0ikx}\nsubject to \u222bh(x)dx=1 via constraints:\n   \u2211_{k=-N}^{N} a_k = 1/2\n   \u2211_{k=-N}^{N} (1-2k\u03c0i) a_k = 0\n\nThen minimize:\n   max_{k\u2208Z} \u222c h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nThis formulation allows us to express the correlation operator using Poisson summation, which we approximate using finite Fourier transforms.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Initialization:\n   - Set initial a_k coefficients using normalized sinc functions\n   - Projection: enforce constraints via singular value decomposition on the Fourier matrix\n\n   b. Phase 1: Global optimization\n   - Use projected gradient descent on the frequency coefficients\n   - Compute correlation via Poisson summation approximation\n\n   c. Phase 2: Local refinement\n   - Estimate optimal grid resolution m using separated coefficients\n   - Discretize using rotated sine waves based on the recovered structure\n\n   d. Phase 3: Iterative improvement\n   - For increasing m, project current coefficients to larger grids\n   - Incorporate recent progress in Erd\u0151s constant computations\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   a. Hyperparameter tuning:\n   - Variational parameter \u03b2 for Fourier coefficients (0.5 to 1.0)\n   - Initial frequency range N (50\u2013200)\n\n   b. Projection method:\n   - Toggle between SVD projection (exact) and log-sum-exp approximation\n\n   c. Fallback mechanism:\n   - Retain best solution from previous projection level\n\n   d. Adaptive switching:\n   - Switch to sparse representation if correlation shows local minima\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Phase durations: 400s for global optimization, 400s for local refinement\n   - Implement fallback projection on 5-second intervals\n   - Handle invalid solutions: aggressive projection of clipped values\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Compute exact correlation using numpy.correlate\n   - Check feasibility: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h) \u2248 n/2 with tolerance 1e-5\n   - Re-evaluate final candidate at least twice",
